WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.416 --> 00:00:04.790
ERAN FEIGENBAUM: Welcome to
Atmosphere Digital Rethinking

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.220
Security in the Cloud.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.870
I'm Eran Feigenbaum, Director
of Security for Google Apps.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.650
We have this great
panel here of experts

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.690
through all across Google
to talk about security.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.740
Information security
has become such

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.910
a hot topic with the
increase in cyber crime,

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.550
the trends in privacy, the
changes in regulations.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.400
It's something that
businesses can't ignore.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.730
Enterprises all over the world
are concerned about security.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.250
And to have a great conversation
about information security,

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.250
we've brought in four different
experts from all across Google,

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.350
each a subject matter
expert in security

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.650
in their own specific domain.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.260
To my far left here, I have Tim
Willis from our Chrome Security

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:44.850
Team.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.660
Next up is Adrian Ludwig.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.130
Adrian is responsible
for Android security.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.340
To my far right is
Stephan Somoygi Stephan

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.890
is a product manager in
our security and privacy

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.220
engineering team.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.470
To my immediate right
is Suzanne Frye.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.890
She's the Director of
Security, Privacy, and Trust

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:04.459
for Google Apps engineering.

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:08.370
The move of business to cloud
computing has really increased.

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.400
Companies see the
benefits of lower cost,

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.890
but also the ability
to innovate faster

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.800
for users to collaborate.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.120
But one of the big
areas of hesitation

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.840
is security right
Companies are not

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.910
comfortable putting their
own data into the cloud.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.834
How would you respond to
somebody that had that concern?

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.250
SUZANNE FREY: I think we're
seeing a real sea change right

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.260
now with respect to
people understanding

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.627
that the cloud is more secure
than any on-premise solution.

00:01:35.627 --> 00:01:37.710
If you just think about
it, mathematically, you've

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.650
got all these different
on-premise solutions

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.382
and individual teams trying
to do the right thing.

00:01:42.382 --> 00:01:44.340
And with any cloud
provider, you have the locus

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.100
of the best talent, the
best expertise centered

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.370
on making your solutions
secure and your data secure.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:54.060
If you take a look
at our customer base,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.090
we have some of the
world's largest banks.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:59.680
We have some of the most
stringent government customers.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.170
We're FedRAMP certified
here in the US.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.920
And the fact that we
can solve for security

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.139
for all those customers
is a great testimony

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:10.600
to our capabilities.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.880
But in addition, we solve
for something special.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.400
In talking to our
customers, it's

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.560
our ability to innovate
and to bring new ideas

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.930
to bear that help enable them
to be competitive, productive,

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.850
and truly novel, and focus on
the things that matter to them.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.730
That's part of our really
special secret sauce.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.940
And I often say to
people at Google,

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.880
security comes in two forms.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.000
It's both traditional
cybersecurity,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.732
but it's also security against
technological stagnation.

00:02:37.732 --> 00:02:39.190
ERAN FEIGENBAUM:
That's interesting

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.950
because we don't usually think
of that second one very often.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.240
We tend to think of
the data security.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.490
That's great from a
cloud perspective.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.557
How does that change in the
Android or mobile world?

00:02:49.557 --> 00:02:51.140
ADRIAN LUDWIG: I
like that observation

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.820
about being too
focused on security

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.880
to the exclusion of innovation.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.902
I hadn't seen that
phrase that way.

00:02:57.902 --> 00:02:59.610
But I think one of
the changes that we've

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.990
seen in the mobile space
over the last few years

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:05.090
is that companies are
focused, first and foremost,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.541
on innovation, Android being
a great example of that.

00:03:08.541 --> 00:03:10.040
But we've tied into
a security model

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.850
that is how people actually
consume applications

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:14.780
and services.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.737
So we thought about the web
and the sandboxing model that

00:03:18.737 --> 00:03:21.070
was used on the web, and we
incorporated that in the way

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.066
we built applications
sandboxing.

00:03:23.066 --> 00:03:26.400
And I think the consequence
of that is cloud services

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.330
are becoming more
and more important.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.100
Most applications that
are built for Android

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.140
have built for mobile regardless
of your mobile platform

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.820
are really cloud based.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.940
So I think those two are tied
together, because both of them

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.270
are thinking about innovation
first and foremost,

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.655
and the security is sort
of unlock that innovation.

00:03:43.655 --> 00:03:45.280
ERAN FEIGENBAUM: So
it seems like cloud

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.270
is actually and mobile
as a reason for companies

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.280
to move to those environments.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.309
Is there stuff that Google's
doing that others really can't?

00:03:53.309 --> 00:03:55.100
TIM WILLIS: I think
it's important to think

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.020
of the context in which
we're talking in here.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.160
So we have a complex
set of systems

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.570
that we're dealing with today.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:04.280
They get more and more
complex over time.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.760
We also have adversaries
with increasing levels

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:08.410
of sophistication.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.350
So you've got that on one
side, and on the other side,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:16.064
we've got IT managers having
to defend their networks.

00:04:16.064 --> 00:04:17.480
The problem with
defenses you need

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.310
to defend everything
incredibly well.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.270
Attackers only need to find
one hole into your network.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.250
So I think that's where
an advantage of moving

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:27.850
to the cloud is that
you have dedicated teams

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.282
with robust experience.

00:04:29.282 --> 00:04:31.490
I mean, Stephan and I were
talking about this earlier

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.130
that some of the
people who I work with

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.800
wrote my textbooks
in university.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.430
And it's one of
those things that I

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.190
get to work with these experts
and that's all they do.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:40.764
They focus on
security, and that's

00:04:40.764 --> 00:04:42.680
one of the huge benefits,
in my point of view,

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.552
of moving to cloud that's great
while we're protecting data

00:04:45.552 --> 00:04:47.760
while it's at Google but we
have to do a lot of stuff

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.850
to make sure the data
safe when it's not Google

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:51.350
STEPHAN SOMOGYI:
Safe Browsing would

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:52.933
be a good example
of something that we

00:04:52.933 --> 00:04:55.960
can do at very, very large
scale, where we actually

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.760
believe that the
right approach is

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.130
make the entire internet safer.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:02.920
So we build systems that
hunt around and find malware

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.320
and find phishing and then
we go and report this.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.097
And so an individual
consumer can

00:05:08.097 --> 00:05:10.680
benefit from this, because the
web browser will let them know.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.760
In a cloud environment,
enterprises

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.890
can take advantage
of this data as well

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.906
and keep themselves protected,
and we take this approach

00:05:17.906 --> 00:05:19.905
through a number of
different areas, certificate

00:05:19.905 --> 00:05:21.990
of transparency being
another example,

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.330
where we're taking a look
at the internet as a whole

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.769
and finding ways to
keep it safe at scale.

00:05:27.769 --> 00:05:30.310
ERAN FEIGENBAUM: So obviously
in working with various experts

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.310
and then you guys are all
experts in your field,

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.970
right this encourage us to
innovate and come up with lots

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:35.970
of security innovation.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.700
Some of those really
actually go beyond

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.310
and change security and
really move the needle

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.550
from what users are used to.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.776
Maybe you can all give me one of
those examples that we've done.

00:05:45.776 --> 00:05:48.150
SUZANNE FREY: I'd like to talk
a bit about security keys.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.979
Security keys have--
Are you going to--

00:05:50.979 --> 00:05:52.020
TIM WILLIS: Oh, no don't.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.754
[LAUGHING]

00:05:53.754 --> 00:05:55.920
ERAN FEIGENBAUM: Wait you
got my collection with me.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:56.920
You have multicolored ones.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.780
SUZANNE FREY: Nice,
almost Google colors too.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:05:58.920
STEPHAN SOMOGYI: Indeed.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.120
You need a green one.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.430
SUZANNE FREY: These
keys are fabulous.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.770
For the longest
time, we have been

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.700
talking about two-factor
authentication

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:11.380
is critically important for
most organizations to implement.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:15.010
And many customers use Google
Authenticator and other apps

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.450
like that to generate
a one-time pass code.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.380
Those are great.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.530
They're certainly
better than nothing.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.690
However, a hardware-based
security key,

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.930
such as these, just quantum
leaps ahead in terms of they're

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:30.260
not hackable and they really
do protect our customers

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:33.410
from phishing in a
way that basically

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.520
the one-time passwords do not.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.049
So.

00:06:36.049 --> 00:06:38.340
TIM WILLIS: I think it's an
interesting point, Suzanne,

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.910
especially talking about
the not-so-glamorous side

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:42.850
of the things that we do.

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:44.621
One of those things
is encryption for me

00:06:44.621 --> 00:06:46.370
like it may not seem
incredibly innovative

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:48.500
but we're working
really hard to make sure

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.350
that all of our
traffic is encrypted

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:52.750
at rest and at transit,
and one example

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.300
where we're being open with
that is our HTTPS transparency

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:56.150
report.

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:58.540
Now, you can go to that site
and you can see our progress

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.540
towards our goal of 100%
encryption in transit

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.960
through all of our products.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:08.550
Again, another example would
be working with TLS 1.3.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.190
That's the next generation
of transport security.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.470
Now, it may not sound
glamorous, but we're not only

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.180
helping to implement
that, we're helping author

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:18.410
the next version, so that
shows that we're in the mix

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.530
and we know what technologies
are around the corner.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:24.650
A practical application of that
would be progressive web apps.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:27.464
So these low friction
web applications

00:07:27.464 --> 00:07:29.380
which are designed to
help increase engagement

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:34.419
and have an app-like experience
for customers and businesses.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:36.460
We've seen studies how
that increases engagement.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.380
And it's fantastic.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.937
It's easy across the board.

00:07:38.937 --> 00:07:40.020
Why am I talking about it?

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:43.946
Well, TLS is actually a hard
requirement for those apps.

00:07:43.946 --> 00:07:45.320
So it's one of
these things where

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.040
not only are we innovating,
we're making sure that security

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.360
is baked in from the get go.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.100
And I think that's one
huge advantage of Google.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:52.200
ADRIAN LUDWIG: There's a
couple elements about that

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:52.790
that are interesting to me.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.320
One of them is it's not so
much that the security itself

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.730
is innovative, it's about
using an innovative product

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.995
to make security available.

00:07:59.995 --> 00:08:02.120
One of the things that we
did early on with Android

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.490
is we thought about the
platform stack and go OK,

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.990
you need to have verified boot,
you need to have encryption,

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.156
you need to have sandboxing.

00:08:08.156 --> 00:08:10.500
Those are all, I think at
this point, almost commodities

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:11.539
for an operating system.

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:13.580
But one of the things that
Google brought to bear

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:15.350
was security services.

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.360
It's going to be a
cloud-connected device,

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.820
and we're going to make
all of those services

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.500
available by default
on all of the devices.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.000
And so we started
thinking about how

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.170
do you bind services into
the operating system itself.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.420
So we added things like
safety net and verify apps

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:33.289
where there are effectively
hooks in the operating

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:35.610
system where we can make
sure that we're adding

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:37.429
security dynamically over time.

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:40.210
So we can innovate in
security even more quickly

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:43.054
than we can innovate in the
operating system itself.

00:08:43.054 --> 00:08:44.720
Does anybody know
that that's happening?

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.130
Not really.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:47.820
But that's OK,
because they're safer

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.570
and they're happier
as a result of it.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.800
So we the same way that
you guys are dealing

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:54.780
with TLS and progress web
apps, binding those two things

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:56.810
together just transparently
in the background.

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:58.200
STEPHAN SOMOGYI: On the
two ends of that spectrum,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.410
there's also transparency.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:01.630
And Tim, you're already talking
with the HTTPS Transparency

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:03.338
Report, but we've been
doing transparency

00:09:03.338 --> 00:09:05.970
and we've been shining light on
ourselves for many, many years.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.360
A couple years back, we did
the Safer E-mail Transparency

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.362
Report, where we were
looking-- first of all,

00:09:10.362 --> 00:09:12.820
is there anything that we're
doing that we could do better?

00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:14.180
And then having
looked at that, what

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:15.850
is the rest of the world
doing, and it's actually

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:17.020
moved the needle forward.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:19.070
We are now in a
position to say yes,

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:22.430
as a result of shining a light
and illuminating the greater

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:24.250
situation on the
internet, more people

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.290
are using encryption
for their e-mail.

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.520
It happens across all of
our transparency reports

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.210
where first we look at ourselves
to see if we're doing the best

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:33.320
job that we possibly can,
and very often then we

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.216
cast our gaze more widely
in the outside world

00:09:36.216 --> 00:09:37.840
to see what else is
out there and if we

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.264
can help people improve things.

00:09:39.264 --> 00:09:40.180
ERAN FEIGENBAUM: Yeah.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:41.700
Transparency actually
a really big part

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:42.710
of moving to the cloud.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:45.100
Especially we see that a lot
with our enterprise customers,

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:45.420
right?

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:45.980
I mean.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.140
SUZANNE FREY: Absolutely.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.640
If you think about
it like anything,

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.120
if you don't know enough or
have sufficient information,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.610
how in the world can
you make a decision?

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:54.860
How can you run your business?

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.750
And I do think that
transparency has

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.520
been one of the biggest
blockers to adoption

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:01.080
across all cloud products.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.160
We've been investing
in multiple areas

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:07.020
to ensure the transparency is
front-and-center to what we do.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:12.154
We engage third-party auditors
to help ensure and provide

00:10:12.154 --> 00:10:13.820
certifications against
certain standards

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.700
like ISO 27017 and 18.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:19.430
Our fed grant process
is also disclosed

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.560
to the government and
leveraged uniquely

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.760
in terms of making sure
that across our company

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:28.590
we are aware of vulnerabilities
and addressing them quickly.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.750
And I think just in general
providing information

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.830
to the end user
about whether or not

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.990
their employees have
had a password violation

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.160
or have had some
suspicious activity

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:40.190
or giving them greater
reporting detail.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.440
And I'm the first to say
that we still have some work

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.055
to do in the
reporting front, and I

00:10:44.055 --> 00:10:46.430
think every cloud provider
does, but we're working really

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:50.461
hard to improve the degree that
an administrator understands

00:10:50.461 --> 00:10:52.460
everything that's happening
within their company

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:54.170
and can take appropriate action.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:55.545
ERAN FEIGENBAUM:
So a lot of what

00:10:55.545 --> 00:10:57.730
you guys all talked about,
whether it's transparency

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:01.550
for our enterprise customers
or for all users or some

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.050
of the innovation in
Android, we really wouldn't

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:06.240
have expected years ago.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:08.360
I, at least, wouldn't
have predicted that.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:11.160
You guys are all experts
and very deep in your field.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:12.670
What would be your
security vision?

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:13.910
What's going to change?

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:16.840
What's going to make our
lives easier for our users?

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:18.830
STEPHAN SOMOGYI: I think
a lot of the security,

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:20.830
as we've talked before,
needs to stay invisible,

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:23.360
and in some cases, can be a
little bit counter-intuitive.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:24.859
So a couple months
back, we released

00:11:24.859 --> 00:11:27.677
a product called OnHub, which
is a home networking router.

00:11:27.677 --> 00:11:29.510
And one of the things
that it's particularly

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:30.850
counter-intuitive
about it but which

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.540
is adding a lot more security
is the fact it's cloud managed.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.722
So all the technology that we
devise to protect our users

00:11:36.722 --> 00:11:39.180
accounts-- like Suzanne was
talking about with the security

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:42.530
key-- all of that benefits
the user because the Google

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:44.360
account is the way
the user starts

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:46.530
to manage their device at home.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:51.070
So as we connect all the various
pieces of our security puzzle,

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:53.110
they all work together
and suddenly you

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.350
have a device at home
which is actually

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.870
cloud managed and
cloud secured, which

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.960
is not the way it was
even a few years ago.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.250
There's a distinct
security benefit now.

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:03.840
ADRIAN LUDWIG: I'd like to talk
a little bit about the elephant

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:05.660
in the room with respect
to security, which

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:10.390
is this dynamic tension
between access to data

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:12.580
and keeping it secure.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:14.770
One of the very hard
things for most enterprises

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.230
is to say listen,
someone else is

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:20.560
going to be the governor of
our data-- steward of our data,

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.527
perhaps I should say,
and what is that company

00:12:22.527 --> 00:12:23.610
going to do with our data?

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:26.340
And I reference audit
reports and certifications

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.120
that we commit to every year.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:31.150
However, machine learning is
one of the most exciting things

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.510
that's happening in the field
of computer science today.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:38.510
And machine learning requires
training data to perform.

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:41.540
And obviously, with customers
sharing data with us,

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.770
we could just take
that data and go

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.360
nuts, which would be
irresponsible of Google

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.850
and would really kill
our vision overall.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.690
But I do think
solving that problem

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:53.190
and putting our
customers in control

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:56.790
of what data we can use to
enhance the way that they work,

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:59.030
to improve productivity,
to reduce the cost,

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.280
and to understand how
competitive they are

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.170
in a global landscape for
a given industry-- that's

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:05.670
super-exciting stuff.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:09.090
So I think finding that gentle
balance between making sure

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:12.690
that customers can maintain
absolute control over who

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:15.130
sees their data, when,
and uses it for what,

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.380
is one of the most exciting
problems we have to solve,

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:21.000
because it's going to bring
tremendous advances to mankind.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:21.875
ERAN FEIGENBAUM: Wow.

00:13:21.875 --> 00:13:22.625
That's impressive.

00:13:22.625 --> 00:13:24.583
TIM WILLIS: I feel like
I'm the guy who's going

00:13:24.583 --> 00:13:26.310
to talk about the boring thing.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.480
I think it's really exciting
that more companies are moving,

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:31.637
I think, more deeply
into the cloud.

00:13:31.637 --> 00:13:33.470
For some background,
before I joined Google,

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:36.010
I was the sysadmin
and an IT manager,

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:37.630
and so I caught
the security bug--

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:40.810
pun intended-- and ended up
joining an incidence response

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:41.310
team.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.190
So a lot of what I was
doing was rolling out

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:46.240
to companies and departments who
were being hacked and helping

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:48.710
them clean up their networks
helping them rebuild up

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:51.274
their defenses and kicking
the miscreants out.

00:13:51.274 --> 00:13:53.690
And it's one of these things
where as we mentioned earlier

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:55.815
it's just going to get more
and more sophisticated,

00:13:55.815 --> 00:13:58.210
and it's harder that
moving to the cloud

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:01.060
actually buys you the Google's
security team in a sense.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:03.160
It gives you the protection
that we're offering.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:06.300
It lets you not have
to worry about that.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:10.415
And one of the great advantages
of that is as an IT manager,

00:14:10.415 --> 00:14:11.790
I don't have to
worry about that.

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.870
I can sleep a little
bit better at night

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.371
knowing that Google's got my
back when it comes to security.

00:14:16.371 --> 00:14:18.995
ADRIAN LUDWIG: I think it's been
interesting talking with folks

00:14:18.995 --> 00:14:20.960
and getting a little bit
different perspective.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:24.860
Even in the last minute or
two, we've heard discussion

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.440
about hardware security,
and we talked about machine

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.020
learning, and pulling in
data from different places.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:33.550
Google every conversation you
learn a little bit something

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:34.154
new.

00:14:34.154 --> 00:14:35.820
And I think that's
one of the key things

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.290
that the security industry
is going through right now.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.920
We're beginning to a
point where there's

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:43.049
enough maturation in
all of our products,

00:14:43.049 --> 00:14:45.090
where there's enough data
that's being pulled in,

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:46.605
enough ability to
analyze that data,

00:14:46.605 --> 00:14:49.430
where we can actually start
to make good decisions.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:53.330
For generations, really,
from a technology standpoint,

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.650
we've been relying
on assumptions

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.250
about what good
security looked like,

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:59.208
which things you need to
be worried about, what

00:14:59.208 --> 00:15:00.292
risk is likely to hit you.

00:15:00.292 --> 00:15:02.124
But now that people are
moving to the cloud,

00:15:02.124 --> 00:15:04.280
now that we have operating
systems like Android

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:05.821
where we're trying
to deploy security

00:15:05.821 --> 00:15:08.530
solutions across the
entire ecosystem,

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:09.990
we get a lot of data.

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:11.640
We can analyze that,
and we can start

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:13.120
to make really good decisions.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.670
So I think that's
the thing that I'm

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:16.350
most excited about
over the next few years

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:17.766
is we're going to
get to the point

00:15:17.766 --> 00:15:19.860
where as professionals,
we can actually

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:23.097
understand risk and make really
good risk-based decisions,

00:15:23.097 --> 00:15:25.430
which isn't something we've
been able to do in the past,

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:27.180
and it's not something
that is possible

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.424
if you're not in the cloud,
if you're not on mobile.

00:15:29.424 --> 00:15:31.090
So that's what I'm
really excited about.

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:31.630
ERAN FEIGENBAUM:
The one thing that's

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.510
really in common with
everything everybody

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:36.420
said is having a large
security team that's capable

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.810
and has the expertise to
really ingest all that,

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.270
to be able to do the
things for you on the cloud

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:44.180
or to use all that
data that now we

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.600
have to make products
more and more secure

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:48.940
or to develop that
machine learning.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.590
So hopefully, this was a
good insight for everybody

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.330
to hear what some of our
security leaders at Google

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.180
are thinking about,
what they're working on,

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:58.360
and what they're working
on in the future.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.270
So thank everybody
for taking the time

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.400
and listening in to us.

